# identity-vocabulary — EXAMPLE. Every entry below is invented.
#
# ⚠ COPY THIS FILE OUT OF THE REPOSITORY BEFORE YOU EDIT IT.
#   cp templates/identity-vocabulary.example ~/.colab/identity-vocabulary
#   chmod 600 ~/.colab/identity-vocabulary        # it is a list of things you consider sensitive
#
# A filled-in vocabulary is a precise index of what an organisation does not want found. It
# is more useful to a reader than any single one of the strings in it, so it never belongs in
# a repository — public or private, tracked or "just for now". The scanner takes it BY PATH
# for exactly this reason (templates/pre-commit-identity), and the audit's metadata check
# reads it the same way.
#
# FORMAT
#   • One term per line. Leading and trailing whitespace is ignored.
#   • Blank lines, and lines whose first non-space character is `#`, are comments. A `#`
#     inside a term is part of the term — comments are whole-line only.
#   • Matching is CASE-INSENSITIVE and by SUBSTRING. `build-box-01` matches
#     `ssh://build-box-01.example.invalid/`, and it also matches `my-build-box-01x`.
#     Substring is the deliberate choice: a hostname or handle appears inside URLs, paths
#     and identifiers with no word boundary anywhere near it, and over-blocking has a
#     one-command override while under-blocking has none.
#   • A term shorter than 3 characters is refused as a mistake — it would match everything.
#   • A line starting with `re:` is an extended regular expression, matched against the
#     lower-cased line. Use it for shapes rather than strings; prefer plain terms otherwise.
#
# WHAT BELONGS HERE — the classes a secret scanner cannot catch, because none of them is a
# credential: machine and host names, internal domains, account handles, absolute home
# paths, customer and partner names, internal service or project code names.
#
# WHAT DOES NOT BELONG HERE: passwords, tokens, keys. Those are secrets — your secret
# scanner already finds them by shape, without being told, and writing one down here to
# "make sure" copies it into a second file that is easier to forget about.

# --- machines and internal hosts -----------------------------------------------------
build-box-01
build-box-02
db-primary.corp.invalid
vpn.corp.invalid

# --- internal domains ----------------------------------------------------------------
corp.invalid
intranet.example

# --- account handles -----------------------------------------------------------------
j-doe-ops
release-bot-7

# --- customers and partners (a commercial disclosure, not a technical one) ------------
Northwind Freight
Blue Harbour Logistics

# --- absolute home paths, as a shape rather than a list ------------------------------
# Matches /Users/<anyone>/ and /home/<anyone>/ — an absolute path names the person as
# surely as their handle does, and it is the single most-copied thing in an error message.
re:/(users|home)/[a-z0-9._-]+/

# --- an internal project code name ---------------------------------------------------
project-lighthouse
